read_string: do not outgrow the caller's buffer (GHSA-f72v-7gmh-m9mj)

Part of GHSA-f72v-7gmh-m9mj, fixed upstream in Hamlib 4.7.2: when the first
byte read from the rig was a backslash, read_string_generic() raised rxmax to
five times the size it had been given, and whatever is at the rig's end of
the line (a serial device, or the peer of a network rig) could write past the
caller's buffer.

diff --git a/src/iofunc.c b/src/iofunc.c
--- a/src/iofunc.c
+++ b/src/iofunc.c
@@ -1377,8 +1377,10 @@ static int read_string_generic(hamlib_port_t *p,
             return -RIG_EIO;
         }
 
-        // check to see if our string startis with \...if so we need more chars
-        if (total_count == 0 && rxbuffer[total_count] == '\\') { rxmax = (rxmax - 1) * 5; }
+        /* [RADE] GHSA-f72v-7gmh-m9mj: a reply that started with a backslash
+         * used to raise rxmax to five times the size of the caller's buffer,
+         * and the rig's end of the line could then write past it (removed
+         * in Hamlib 4.7.2 as well). */
 
         total_count += (int) rd_count;
 
